{"summary":"Accept all three. The registry recomputation concern is real, but the safe fix is to share one fresh registry through the breakdown rather than assume `state.habitat_registry` is always current. The denitrification comment is backwards. The ROL change also altered the E4a baseline by deleting rooted O2 demand, which conflates base demand with the new bonus parameter.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-6-5c9d4bed","decision":"accept","rationale":"`root_oxygenation_active_biomass_g()` sits in the substrate hot path and rebuilding the habitat registry there is redundant. More importantly, `substrate_oxygenation_breakdown()` already mixes registry sources (`state.habitat_registry` for substrate demand, recomputation for root activity), so threading a single registry snapshot through the calculation would be cleaner and more consistent. The implementation should prefer a shared fresh registry parameter rather than blindly trusting `state.habitat_registry` in every call path."},{"amendment_id":"fr-6-8b38a232","decision":"accept","rationale":"The comment is scientifically and mechanically backwards. A deeper oxic zone leaves less suboxic volume, so hourly denitrification should decrease, which is exactly what the later test asserts. Leaving the current wording in place invites future changes in the wrong direction."},{"amendment_id":"fr-6-e14eeefd","decision":"accept","rationale":"This change did more than add an additive ROL modifier: it removed the preexisting rooted-biomass O2 demand from the E4a base term. That means `rol_rate_cm_per_g = 0` no longer represents \"no ROL bonus\"; it also erases the prior rooted-plant substrate demand effect. That conflates two opposing mechanisms into one knob, changes the baseline model semantics, and makes calibration and reasoning less inspectable."}]}